# Ontology Console — environment

# Server-only. Three modes, by these two variables:
#
# 1. Neither set → the console serves its in-memory demo data from the Next route
#    handlers under /api/ontology (no backend, no auth).
# 2. ONTOLOGY_API_URL set → every /api/ontology/* call is forwarded to that one
#    backend with the caller's `Authorization: Token <dm_token>` (stored by the
#    ibl.ai SSO flow).
# ONTOLOGY_API_URL=http://localhost:8000
# 3. ONTOLOGY_MULTI_PLATFORM=true (and no ONTOLOGY_API_URL) → one console for every
#    platform, as at ontology.iblai.me. Each call goes to the backend of the signed-in
#    admin's own platform: the address the platform keeps for it, read with the admin's
#    token. It must be a public https address. An admin of a platform without one gets
#    "Connect a backend" (the install command, a key for the backend, the address); a
#    member who is not an admin gets a notice. `ontology platform address --url https://…`
#    on the server records the same address.
# ONTOLOGY_MULTI_PLATFORM=true
#    For platforms that keep no ontology records yet, add ONTOLOGY_BACKEND_STORE=browser:
#    the address is then kept by each admin's browser (localStorage) and named on every
#    call, and the console uses it once the platform knows the sign-in and the address
#    answers as a backend that takes it. Each browser connects once; nothing is written to
#    the platform. Remove it when the platform keeps the record, and connect once more.
# ONTOLOGY_BACKEND_STORE=browser
#
# The browser never calls a backend directly, so no CORS.

# ibl.ai SSO (wired via /iblai-vibe-auth)
# Leave NEXT_PUBLIC_MAIN_TENANT_KEY unset in mode 3: the platform is the one signed in to.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_MAIN_TENANT_KEY=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_PLATFORM_BASE_DOMAIN=iblai.app
# NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=https://api.iblai.app
# NEXT_PUBLIC_AUTH_URL=https://login.iblai.app
